Some exclusions apply.

00:00:07.159 --> 00:00:09.210
- That means I'll expect you

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:13.050
to be at work and in
court as the She-Hulk.

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:15.150
- No, no, no.

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:17.580
Absolutely not. Not going to happen.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:20.931
This is an employment
lawyer's worst nightmare.

00:00:20.931 --> 00:00:24.330
(bright music)

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:26.070
Okay, today, we are looking at "She-Hulk."

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:27.870
Now I really want to like this show,

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:29.820
but so far, from the
little bit that I've seen,

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.640
my expectations about legal
realism are pretty low,

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:33.600
but, today, let's look at

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.660
the first half of Season 1 of "She-Hulk."

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:38.850
- The defendant used
their limitless resources,

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:41.670
political and monetary, for financial gain

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:43.800
at the expense of public safety,

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.936
causing the deaths of innocent people.

00:00:45.936 --> 00:00:46.800
- [Speaker] Oh, that's so wordy.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.390
- They did this because they believed

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:50.400
they would not be held accountable,

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:54.780
that their power shielded them
from very real consequences,

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:58.710
but, today, you, the jury,
can prove that's not the case.

00:00:58.710 --> 00:00:59.970
- All right, off to a strong start.

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:00.803
We can see already that

00:01:00.803 --> 00:01:03.330
Jen Walters has two degrees from UCLA,

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:05.130
one for undergrad and one for law school.

00:01:05.130 --> 00:01:07.590
You know who else who has
two degrees from UCLA?

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:08.820
Your boy, LegalEagle.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:10.860
Now, I can neither confirm nor or deny

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:14.512
that I also have incredible superpowers.

00:01:14.512 --> 00:01:16.050
We'll find out later.

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:20.730
- I ask you to look only
at the facts of this case.

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:25.230
You'll see that my client
was well within his right

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:27.993
to protect his business interest.

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:33.210
These accidental deaths were
an unfortunate byproduct

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:36.030
of an ambitious expansion plan.

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:38.970
Did my client know lives
would be in danger?

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:42.613
That is debatable and depends
on your definition of knowing.

00:01:42.613 --> 00:01:45.720
- Okay, so this is a
terrible closing argument.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:48.134
It's basically just a word
salad of legal concepts

00:01:48.134 --> 00:01:51.540
thrown together in a way that
is not persuasive at all,

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:54.960
and the craziest thing here
is that this is the defense,

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.900
which for some reason in
this alternate universe,

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:00.780
the defense gives their closing
argument in a criminal case

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:01.860
before the prosecution.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:04.620
That is not how this works.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:06.480
The prosecution has the burden of proof,

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:09.510
and they always go first in
terms of closing arguments.

00:02:09.510 --> 00:02:11.970
So it makes absolutely
no sense that Jen Walters

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:14.670
has not given her closing
argument before the defense has,

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:16.485
but that's pretty far down

00:02:16.485 --> 00:02:18.660
in terms of legal
inaccuracies at this point.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:21.960
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, so
this chick, pretty decent,

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:24.513
turned into a Hulk, like a chick Hulk.

00:02:25.603 --> 00:02:26.553
- A She-Hulk?

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:28.463
- Exactly.

00:02:28.463 --> 00:02:30.810
- [Speaker] She-Hulk, yeah!

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:32.220
- Okay, so I really enjoy

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:35.413
the supposed name of this
lawyer bar, the Legal Ease.

00:02:35.413 --> 00:02:37.830
The thing is, though,
despite this being a trope

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:40.500
in lots of different TV
shows, in my experience,

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:41.760
lawyers don't congregate

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:46.470
in some nearby bar to the
courthouse after hours.

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:51.470
In my experience, lawyers
tend to drink alone at home.

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:52.950
It's actually kind of a problem.

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:55.110
Substance abuse is a
real thing among lawyers.

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:56.520
- We didn't win the case.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:57.523
- Oh, yeah, we did.

00:02:57.523 --> 00:02:59.520
- GLK &amp; H had it declared a mistrial.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:04.050
- Okay, so a mistrial was
declared. That's not crazy.

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:06.270
The standard for mistrial is that

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:09.660
the defendant cannot receive a fair trial.

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:11.130
The chances of receiving a fair trial

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:13.140
are irreparably damaged.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:16.023
The thing is, though, when
a mistrial is declared,

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:18.120
there's gonna be a do-over.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:19.920
They're going to have another trial.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:22.890
You don't lose the case. The
defendant doesn't walk free.

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:26.370
It's just that this particular
jury has to be dismissed

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:27.900
and you have to do it all over again.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:28.733
- Just the same.

00:03:28.733 --> 00:03:30.690
Since you saved the lives
of the entire jury today,

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:33.900
GLK &amp; H argued that the jury
was then biased in your favor.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:35.220
- Well, what was I supposed to do?

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:37.350
Like, let them get hit
in the face with the desk

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:38.910
and then they have blunt force trauma

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:41.160
and they're all bleeding out
all over the courtroom floor?

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:44.970
- No, you did the right thing,
but it unraveled our case.

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:48.960
- It didn't unravel their
case, and, if anything,

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:52.050
it would be more reason
for her to be counsel

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:53.850
on the next trial,

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:55.890
because she's gotten
all this publicity that,

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:57.510
not only was she a lawyer,

00:03:57.510 --> 00:04:00.210
but she also saved the lives of the jury,

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:04.140
so the idea that her district
attorney boss is upset

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:05.610
is preposterous.

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:08.220
- Jen, I'm sorry. I have to let you go.

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:10.020
Well, this makes you a
liability for the DA's office.

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:12.240
- What? Okay, all right.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:13.830
Preposterous that they would be upset

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:15.390
that they lost the case
because of mistrial,

00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:17.040
which isn't losing the case,

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:21.960
but double or triple so
that they're firing her

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:25.260
because she transformed into
She-Hulk and saved the jury.

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:26.370
There's an argument here

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:28.590
that this is a violation of the ADA.

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:30.840
The Americans with Disabilities
Act prohibits discrimination

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:33.210
against people with disabilities
in terms of employment,

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:35.790
and the Civil Rights Act of
1964 prohibits discrimination

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:36.930
on the basis of race, color,

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:38.940
religion, sex, or national origin,

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:41.670
and, here, she has a genetic abnormality

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:43.200
that makes her a superhero.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:44.730
There are all kinds of issues here,

00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:46.890
and, also, I mean, this is a small thing,

00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:50.850
but, often, district
attorneys' offices have unions,

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:53.190
so there would be a whole
grievance procedure.

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:54.600
She's a government employee,

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:57.090
so she receives all kinds
of due process protections.

00:04:57.090 --> 00:04:59.730
The manner in which she
was fired, impossible.

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:01.830
Absolutely impossible. Ridiculous.

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:04.424
It's just a plot device to
get her to go somewhere else.

00:05:04.424 --> 00:05:05.940
(superhero transition music)

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:07.320
- Oh, hey, Devin.

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:09.840
Nando here. Just wanted
to clear something up.

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:12.060
That plot point makes way more sense

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:14.370
in the comic they're
taking it directly from.

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:15.630
There's a similar moment

00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:17.940
where Jen is fired from her law firm

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:19.980
because, after she saves the jury,

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:22.620
the case Jen won is deemed a mistrial.

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:24.810
However, there are two
pretty big differences

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:26.730
that make the firing work in the book

00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:28.350
better than it does in the show.

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:30.690
One, Jen is a mess.

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:33.210
She had a rough early 2000s,

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:35.040
but, second, more importantly,

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:37.350
because Jen was an Avenger at the time,

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:39.600
the court case in the
book is not interrupted by

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:42.210
Titania trying to mess up that courthouse,

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:46.080
but MODOK, the big evil
head in a floating chair

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:48.240
trying to freeze the entire planet.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:51.150
So I think Jen's boss
has a stronger case here.

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:53.400
This is not an isolated incident.

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:56.520
Everyone on the planet has
been saved by She-Hulk,

00:05:56.520 --> 00:05:59.400
so you could say Jen has undue leverage

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:02.400
over every potential juror in every case,

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:04.599
and therefore anyone arguing against Jen

00:06:04.599 --> 00:06:06.120
could get a mistrial

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:08.657
on the basis that Jen
saved those jurors too.

00:06:08.657 --> 00:06:09.575
(superhero transition music)

00:06:09.575 --> 00:06:11.790
- I'd like to offer you a job.

00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:15.120
Ms. Walters, you laid out
a winning case against us

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:18.390
before I had it declared
a mistrial, of course.

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:21.180
It's no easy feat against GLK &amp; H,

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:24.120
so I would like you to come work for us.

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:26.430
- Okay, this is slightly less unrealistic,

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:28.470
and frankly I think this
probably would happen.

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:30.750
In the wake of Jen
Walters becoming She-Hulk

00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:31.920
and saving the entire jury

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:35.550
and presumably having
above-average legal skills,

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:36.780
because she went to UCLA Law,

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:39.390
I can absolutely see that
some law firm would reach out

00:06:39.390 --> 00:06:41.790
and want to capitalize
on that good publicity.

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:44.760
So, you know, I think that's a cool idea.

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:47.100
- You got me fired and
now you wanna hire me?

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:49.440
- To be head of a new division, yes.

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:50.273
Take some time to think-

00:06:50.273 --> 00:06:51.106
- I accept,

00:06:52.229 --> 00:06:55.950
but I will only do it

00:06:55.950 --> 00:07:00.030
if I can hire my own
paralegal, non-negotiable.

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:02.760
- I truly do not care
who your paralegal is.

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:03.593
- Yes.

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:06.333
- Okay, that's a great line.

00:07:06.333 --> 00:07:09.990
Would some senior partner care
about her hiring a paralegal?

00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:12.540
Absolutely not whatsoever.

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:13.620
Kind of a ridiculous condition.

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:16.050
I would want to know how
much I was being paid.

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:17.850
I would want to know if I was

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:21.450
a capital or equity
partner in the law firm.

00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:23.220
I would want to know if I get

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:28.220
a percentage of the law firm's
revenue, because, you know,

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:30.208
unclear how much actual lawyering

00:07:30.208 --> 00:07:32.070
she's going to be able to do,

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:34.920
so if they want to
capitalize on her notoriety,

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:36.674
and there are lots of
partners in law firms

00:07:36.674 --> 00:07:38.264
that don't actually do much practicing.

00:07:38.264 --> 00:07:40.440
They are there to be rainmakers

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:43.620
and get people to come in the door.

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:45.325
You know, she should get a percentage of

00:07:45.325 --> 00:07:47.190
the profits of that law firm.

00:07:47.190 --> 00:07:49.170
She has negotiated none of these things.

00:07:49.170 --> 00:07:51.690
You would expect her to do so.

00:07:51.690 --> 00:07:53.933
I am questioning her business skills,

00:07:53.933 --> 00:07:55.860
if not her legal skills at this point.

00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:59.940
- We just started a superhuman
law division and I want you,

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:03.210
well, the She-Hulk, to be the face of it.

00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:04.043
- Oh.

00:08:04.043 --> 00:08:07.590
- Yeah, okay, so in a world
where superheroes are real,

00:08:07.590 --> 00:08:09.085
I can absolutely see law firms

00:08:09.085 --> 00:08:11.343
specializing in superhero law.

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:15.240
You know, this particular
YouTube channel has covered

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:18.543
a lot of the implications
of superheroes being,

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:23.430
shall we say, legally controversial,

00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:25.380
and I can absolutely believe that

00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:27.450
there would be a million different things

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:29.970
that not only superheroes
would need to have covered

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:31.950
from a legal perspective,
but also, you know,

00:08:31.950 --> 00:08:36.060
victims of superhero crime
or supervillain crime

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:39.450
and the aftermath of
superheroes saving the day,

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:41.280
but also causing lots and lots of damage.

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:44.670
So, yeah, I think a superhero law division

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:46.950
is a really neat idea.

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:48.510
A little upset that

00:08:48.510 --> 00:08:50.400
they didn't call me
first about that though.

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:52.763
- That means I'll expect you to be at work

00:08:52.763 --> 00:08:56.580
and in court as the She-Hulk.

00:08:56.580 --> 00:08:57.624
- Right now?

00:08:57.624 --> 00:08:59.654
- [Holden] Mhm.

00:08:59.654 --> 00:09:01.074
- Um.

00:09:01.074 --> 00:09:02.610
- [Holden] Go ahead.

00:09:02.610 --> 00:09:05.940
- No, no, no, absolutely not.

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:07.170
Not going to happen.

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:10.650
This is an employment
lawyer's worst nightmare

00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:13.260
that some partner at the firm,

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:15.600
some old white guy is demanding that

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:17.878
Jen Walters turn into She-Hulk

00:09:17.878 --> 00:09:20.639
as a condition of her employment.

00:09:20.639 --> 00:09:21.930
Absolutely not.

00:09:21.930 --> 00:09:26.400
You know, there are places
that can require you

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:28.080
to dress in a certain way.

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:30.030
Hooters is the classic example,

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:33.360
that there is a sexual
nature to the restaurant,

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:35.994
and so they have been allowed to require

00:09:35.994 --> 00:09:40.350
certain sexualized uniforms
and things of that nature.

00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:42.572
- Read the sign, pervert.

00:09:42.572 --> 00:09:43.770
- [Homer] D'oh!

00:09:43.770 --> 00:09:47.130
- A law firm doesn't have that purpose,

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:50.040
and you can't have differing requirements

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:52.320
between men and women
or a specific person.

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:54.190
That's crazy, especially given

00:09:55.500 --> 00:09:59.520
the Rule 34 nature of She-Hulk in general.

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:00.963
Do not Google that.

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:03.810
Yeah, I mean, this is not going to fly.

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:06.457
Now, arguably, the law firm would say,

00:10:06.457 --> 00:10:07.650
"Well, we hired you

00:10:07.650 --> 00:10:10.590
because you have notoriety as She-Hulk

00:10:10.590 --> 00:10:12.510
and we want She-Hulk to be the lawyer."

00:10:12.510 --> 00:10:14.850
The thing is everybody knows that

00:10:14.850 --> 00:10:17.910
her identity of Jen Walters
is synonymous with She-Hulk,

00:10:17.910 --> 00:10:19.890
so I don't think they
have a good argument.

00:10:19.890 --> 00:10:21.930
- Mr. Blonsky, I was expecting-

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:22.830
- The Abomination, right?

00:10:22.830 --> 00:10:24.630
You won't be seeing much of him about.

00:10:24.630 --> 00:10:28.170
Honestly, I've completely
transformed myself.

00:10:28.170 --> 00:10:33.170
You know, physically,
obviously, but also spiritually.

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:35.531
- Okay, I think this is a really neat idea

00:10:35.531 --> 00:10:37.590
in the world of superhero law.

00:10:37.590 --> 00:10:41.490
You have a former
supervillain who was captured,

00:10:41.490 --> 00:10:45.930
put in jail, in a supermax
jail, and served out his time.

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:48.870
Now, query whether he actually
would get out of jail,

00:10:48.870 --> 00:10:50.910
given that I think a lot of his charges,

00:10:50.910 --> 00:10:51.743
it's been a long time

00:10:51.743 --> 00:10:54.016
since I've seen the original "Hulk" movie,

00:10:54.016 --> 00:10:56.820
involve a lot of attempted murder.

00:10:56.820 --> 00:10:58.830
So, you know, query whether

00:10:58.830 --> 00:11:00.960
he's actually eligible for parole,

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:04.290
but the thing is, prisons,
theoretically at least,

00:11:04.290 --> 00:11:07.050
are not in the habit of

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:09.390
confining people for
the rest of their lives

00:11:09.390 --> 00:11:13.011
unless the crime itself calls
for them to be incarcerated

00:11:13.011 --> 00:11:16.650
for their entire lives, and
it's entirely possible that,

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:20.040
if all of the charges were
related to property damage,

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:21.840
then, okay, he served his time,

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:23.820
and he might be eligible for parole,

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:26.640
and you can imagine, in
a world of supervillainy,

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:29.040
that people would be uneasy about

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:30.300
a supervillain being released

00:11:30.300 --> 00:11:34.080
back into the rest of
humanity, but the thing is that

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:36.660
the prison shouldn't be
confining him forever,

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:40.680
and this is a really interesting
analogy to real life.

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:43.200
We have people that did something bad,

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:44.730
but the punishment doesn't allow them

00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:46.080
to be confined for the
rest of their lives,

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:47.773
and they have to be
reintegrated eventually.

00:11:47.773 --> 00:11:49.380
So this is a really cool idea.

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:51.300
I like that they're exploring this.

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:53.220
- So far, there have
been no statements made

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:56.460
by Emil Blonsky's attorney
after shocking footage leaked,

00:11:56.460 --> 00:11:58.620
showing the Abomination participating in

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:01.320
what appears to be an
underground fight club

00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:04.290
after having somehow escaped from prison.

00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:05.123
Oh.

00:12:05.123 --> 00:12:08.160
Do you understand that this
constitutes another crime

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:10.320
and totally destroys
your chances at parole?

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:11.430
- What?

00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:13.080
- Okay, so that's accurate.

00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:16.830
If a prison inmate
decided to leave prison,

00:12:16.830 --> 00:12:18.480
that would be considered a separate crime,

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:21.180
though you would have to
criminally prosecute them

00:12:21.180 --> 00:12:22.380
for that separate crime.

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:24.330
It wouldn't just be up
to the prison to say,

00:12:24.330 --> 00:12:25.260
you escaped from prison

00:12:25.260 --> 00:12:26.760
and we're tacking on, you know,

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:28.140
10 years to your sentence.

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:31.110
- I was forced to leave my cell,

00:12:31.110 --> 00:12:33.540
but I returned of my own free will.

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:35.610
- Who forced you to escape.

00:12:35.610 --> 00:12:40.411
- Sorcerer Supreme of the
Mystic Arts, and his name is-

00:12:40.411 --> 00:12:42.840
- Wong. Just Wong.

00:12:42.840 --> 00:12:45.953
- Okay, so I think what's
gonna happen here is that

00:12:45.953 --> 00:12:49.740
this is going to involve
the parole aspect of it.

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:51.090
You would imagine that

00:12:51.090 --> 00:12:54.450
someone trying to get
out of jail, to escape,

00:12:54.450 --> 00:12:58.132
would reflect negatively
on a parole board hearing.

00:12:58.132 --> 00:12:59.040
- What?

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:02.370
- And, here, Emil Blonsky is
going to try to ameliorate that

00:13:02.370 --> 00:13:04.140
by saying that he didn't try to escape.

00:13:04.140 --> 00:13:06.090
He was forced to escape by Wong.

00:13:06.090 --> 00:13:09.057
- We do have a witness who
is able to clear Mr. Blonsky

00:13:09.057 --> 00:13:11.250
of any wrongdoing on his part.

00:13:11.250 --> 00:13:13.260
- Then we'll start with that then.

00:13:13.260 --> 00:13:18.240
- Well, yes, the witness is
prepared to give a statement.

00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:20.790
- Okay, that is completely ridiculous

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:22.680
that the stakes here are,

00:13:22.680 --> 00:13:26.670
will Wong show up to this
parole board hearing?

00:13:26.670 --> 00:13:29.790
I mean, I imagine what you would do is

00:13:29.790 --> 00:13:33.570
you would probably want to
have him testify in person,

00:13:33.570 --> 00:13:35.460
but for belts and suspenders,

00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:38.280
you would get a written
declaration from Wong.

00:13:38.280 --> 00:13:39.540
Jennifer Walters would write it up

00:13:39.540 --> 00:13:42.540
and Wong would sign it
under penalty of perjury

00:13:42.540 --> 00:13:44.640
and you would provide
that to the prison board

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:46.560
so that nothing can go wrong,

00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:48.901
to make sure that the
witness is testifying

00:13:48.901 --> 00:13:53.901
that he forced Blonsky
to escape from prison.

00:13:54.240 --> 00:13:57.210
This whole thing about Wong,
will he show up or not,

00:13:57.210 --> 00:13:58.462
is totally contrived.

00:13:58.462 --> 00:14:03.416
(magic whirring)
(dramatic music)

00:14:03.416 --> 00:14:05.970
(audience applauds)

00:14:05.970 --> 00:14:08.028
- Here is a cease and desist.

00:14:08.028 --> 00:14:09.393
- Cease and desist?

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:12.510
What cease and desist?

00:14:12.510 --> 00:14:15.510
- Okay, so I'm not sure on what basis

00:14:15.510 --> 00:14:18.240
they can issue a cease and desist

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:22.143
to stop Donny Blaze from
using this particular magic.

00:14:23.040 --> 00:14:25.020
I mean, look, that has
never stopped a lawyer

00:14:25.020 --> 00:14:26.490
from writing a cease and desist letter.

00:14:26.490 --> 00:14:29.310
We see on the internet all
sorts of terrible lawyers

00:14:29.310 --> 00:14:30.480
write cease and desist letters.

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:34.470
- However, "Dilly Dilly"
is the motto of our realm.

00:14:34.470 --> 00:14:37.140
- So, I guess, in this sense,
this is just like real life.

00:14:37.140 --> 00:14:38.880
You have very dubious circumstances

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:41.520
in which to force someone
to stop doing something

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:44.550
that you don't want them to
do, and yet the lawyer is

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:46.830
writing a cease and desist
letter to threaten them.

00:14:46.830 --> 00:14:48.480
- We have filed a complaint

00:14:48.480 --> 00:14:50.245
and a motion for preliminary injunction

00:14:50.245 --> 00:14:51.600
against the defendant,

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:55.200
citing unfair competition
and gross negligence.

00:14:55.200 --> 00:14:56.280
- I must say, counselor,

00:14:56.280 --> 00:14:57.900
I am hard pressed to believe that

00:14:57.900 --> 00:15:00.660
a magic trick could
constitute gross negligence.

00:15:00.660 --> 00:15:03.720
- Well, okay, yeah,
there is the question of

00:15:03.720 --> 00:15:07.050
whether a magic trick can
constitute gross negligence,

00:15:07.050 --> 00:15:09.630
but the bigger issue here is that

00:15:09.630 --> 00:15:12.630
Wong doesn't have standing to file suit

00:15:12.630 --> 00:15:14.970
on the basis of negligence.

00:15:14.970 --> 00:15:17.190
Negligence is a cause of action

00:15:17.190 --> 00:15:21.630
that requires you to be injured
by the act of negligence.

00:15:21.630 --> 00:15:25.087
So, you know, the young
woman who was sent to,

00:15:25.087 --> 00:15:26.850
you know, the demon realm,

00:15:26.850 --> 00:15:30.210
she has a cause of action
for gross negligence.

00:15:30.210 --> 00:15:33.090
Wong does not. He's a
complete third party.

00:15:33.090 --> 00:15:36.000
Now, with respect to
unfair competition, here,

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:38.070
Wong is not engaged in commerce.

00:15:38.070 --> 00:15:39.810
He's not competing with Donny Blaze,

00:15:39.810 --> 00:15:41.490
so that doesn't work either.

00:15:41.490 --> 00:15:43.590
Also, you would probably
just turn this over

00:15:43.590 --> 00:15:44.423
to the district's attorney.

00:15:44.423 --> 00:15:46.470
You have someone walking around,

00:15:46.470 --> 00:15:49.770
using what is effectively a deadly weapon,

00:15:49.770 --> 00:15:53.310
engaged in all kinds of nefarious acts.

00:15:53.310 --> 00:15:54.990
That's probably something
the district's attorney

00:15:54.990 --> 00:15:57.060
should probably prosecute here.

00:15:57.060 --> 00:16:00.010
- Do you have any witnesses
who can corroborate your claim?

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:03.879
- Yes, we do, your honor.

00:16:03.879 --> 00:16:05.940
(dramatic music)

00:16:05.940 --> 00:16:06.773
- [Speaker] I'm gonna show you all

00:16:06.773 --> 00:16:09.270
my high school dance routine right now.

00:16:09.270 --> 00:16:11.970
- By the way, lack of evidence
is not the problem here.

00:16:11.970 --> 00:16:14.070
You can have tons and tons of evidence,

00:16:14.070 --> 00:16:15.180
but if you don't have a cause of action

00:16:15.180 --> 00:16:17.070
that works in the circumstances,

00:16:17.070 --> 00:16:18.780
this case should be dismissed,

00:16:18.780 --> 00:16:20.850
and, here, this is
exactly the kind of case

00:16:20.850 --> 00:16:23.220
that should be dismissed
on a motion to dismiss.

00:16:23.220 --> 00:16:26.580
- I move to dismiss. You
cannot copyright magic.

00:16:26.580 --> 00:16:28.650
- No.
- There is precedent.

00:16:28.650 --> 00:16:29.940
- According to Jen Walters,

00:16:29.940 --> 00:16:31.860
they're not filing a copyright suit,

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:34.200
because you can't copyright magic.

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:36.050
It's, ugh!

00:16:36.050 --> 00:16:37.413
It's, ugh.

00:16:37.413 --> 00:16:40.170
It's like someone pulled
out a legal dictionary,

00:16:40.170 --> 00:16:43.620
flipped to a random page, threw
some words into the script,

00:16:43.620 --> 00:16:47.130
and then, you know, set it in a courtroom.

00:16:47.130 --> 00:16:52.060
- My client is obviously

00:16:53.700 --> 00:16:56.440
novating this ridiculous

00:16:57.810 --> 00:16:59.670
defalcation.

00:16:59.670 --> 00:17:02.497
This is just a blatant attempt at

00:17:02.497 --> 00:17:07.497
"nemo debet bis vexari
pro una et eadem causa."

00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:10.920
No further questions, your honor.

00:17:10.920 --> 00:17:11.910
- Hello, fellas.

00:17:11.910 --> 00:17:13.230
- Good evening, my lady.

00:17:13.230 --> 00:17:14.880
- Before I send my little friend here

00:17:14.880 --> 00:17:16.290
back to his own dimension,

00:17:16.290 --> 00:17:17.580
I was wondering if you would agree to

00:17:17.580 --> 00:17:19.200
the terms of our cease and desist.

00:17:19.200 --> 00:17:20.606
- Eh, maybe.
- Absolutely.

00:17:20.606 --> 00:17:21.439
(beast snarls)

00:17:21.439 --> 00:17:22.740
- Fine, okay, we agree!

00:17:22.740 --> 00:17:24.990
- My office will be in touch.

00:17:24.990 --> 00:17:25.823
- Yeah, okay.

00:17:25.823 --> 00:17:29.004
So, I mean, forming a contract

00:17:29.004 --> 00:17:31.620
while threatening someone with a demon,

00:17:31.620 --> 00:17:33.360
probably going to constitute duress,

00:17:33.360 --> 00:17:37.350
and whatever terms they have
agreed to is not going to work.

00:17:37.350 --> 00:17:39.180
Basically, this is the
Scoleri Brothers joke

00:17:39.180 --> 00:17:40.860
from "Ghostbusters II."

00:17:40.860 --> 00:17:42.870
- All right, I rescind the order!

00:17:42.870 --> 00:17:43.864
Case dismissed!

00:17:43.864 --> 00:17:44.697
- Hooray, we won the case!

00:17:44.697 --> 00:17:45.993
- Now do something!

00:17:45.993 --> 00:17:49.170
- I've got a special delivery
for a one Jennifer Walters.

00:17:49.170 --> 00:17:51.300
- I recognize a process
server when I see one.

00:17:51.300 --> 00:17:53.130
- Ha, right.

00:17:53.130 --> 00:17:56.700
I've got court documents filed
by Titania Worldwide LLC.

00:17:56.700 --> 00:17:58.500
- Okay, I have to say I really like

00:17:58.500 --> 00:18:00.907
this joke going in the
other direction and saying,

00:18:00.907 --> 00:18:02.385
"Oh, yeah, I totally recognize
you're a process server.

00:18:02.385 --> 00:18:04.140
Just give me the papers."

00:18:04.140 --> 00:18:07.830
So often we hear about people
trying to dodge service.

00:18:07.830 --> 00:18:10.260
There's no point. You're
going to be served eventually.

00:18:10.260 --> 00:18:11.790
It's just driving up court costs.

00:18:11.790 --> 00:18:13.800
- She's suing you for
misuse of a trademark.

00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:15.570
- What trademark?

00:18:15.570 --> 00:18:19.067
She's suing me over the name
She-Hulk? But that's my name.

00:18:19.067 --> 00:18:21.000
- Not if she trademarked it first, right?

00:18:21.000 --> 00:18:22.350
- Oh, is that how it works, your honor?

00:18:22.350 --> 00:18:23.183
- Yeah.

00:18:23.183 --> 00:18:24.016
- Get out of here.

00:18:24.016 --> 00:18:25.170
- Hold on, sorry. Thank you.

00:18:25.170 --> 00:18:28.402
- Spoiler, that is not
exactly how a trademark works.

00:18:28.402 --> 00:18:30.363
Hopefully, we'll talk about that more.

00:18:31.230 --> 00:18:33.420
- I'm so sorry, little child,

00:18:33.420 --> 00:18:35.051
but the line starts way back there.

00:18:35.051 --> 00:18:36.960
_ I don't wanna cut.

00:18:36.960 --> 00:18:40.462
- Okay, so this is Titania,
who is the supervillain

00:18:40.462 --> 00:18:43.830
that burst into the
courtroom in Episode 1,

00:18:43.830 --> 00:18:45.720
and nearly killed half of the jury

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:48.990
by throwing a table at them.

00:18:48.990 --> 00:18:50.855
Why is she not in jail?

00:18:50.855 --> 00:18:52.204
She engaged in

00:18:52.204 --> 00:18:54.033
a whole bunch of accounts
of attempted murder.

00:18:54.033 --> 00:18:55.470
- What seems to be the issue?

00:18:55.470 --> 00:18:59.040
- You're using my name to
sell your trashy products,

00:18:59.040 --> 00:19:00.360
and you're suing me?

00:19:00.360 --> 00:19:01.800
- Get over it, Jennifer.

00:19:01.800 --> 00:19:03.780
The name She-Hulk is mine.

00:19:03.780 --> 00:19:06.600
- So, Titania here is
using the mark She-Hulk

00:19:06.600 --> 00:19:09.630
in commerce with a bunch
of makeup products,

00:19:09.630 --> 00:19:13.020
some of which appear to
have dubious efficacy,

00:19:13.020 --> 00:19:14.670
so that makes sense.

00:19:14.670 --> 00:19:17.610
To get a trademark, you
have to, generally speaking,

00:19:17.610 --> 00:19:19.710
use that mark in commerce,

00:19:19.710 --> 00:19:22.020
which I think tees up a
really interesting idea

00:19:22.020 --> 00:19:23.820
in the world of superhero law,

00:19:23.820 --> 00:19:25.260
because you have all these superheroes

00:19:25.260 --> 00:19:28.020
who are using a name,

00:19:28.020 --> 00:19:30.000
but they're not really
using it in commerce.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:31.590
They're using it to save people.

00:19:31.590 --> 00:19:34.500
You can imagine, Superman,
he would never try to

00:19:34.500 --> 00:19:37.590
commercially capitalize
on the term Superman,

00:19:37.590 --> 00:19:39.540
whereas you can imagine a
whole bunch of other people

00:19:39.540 --> 00:19:41.130
would want to capitalize on that.

00:19:41.130 --> 00:19:44.670
So you can have a situation
where someone is using a name,

00:19:44.670 --> 00:19:47.340
here, She-Hulk, in an altruistic way,

00:19:47.340 --> 00:19:50.580
but someone else is
gonna be first to market

00:19:50.580 --> 00:19:52.980
with some product in commerce,

00:19:52.980 --> 00:19:54.270
and you can only get a trademark

00:19:54.270 --> 00:19:56.250
if you're using something in commerce.

00:19:56.250 --> 00:19:58.350
- You said you didn't like
the name She-Hulk anyways.

00:19:58.350 --> 00:19:59.340
- I don't!

00:19:59.340 --> 00:20:00.173
- Okay.

00:20:00.173 --> 00:20:01.890
- It is just what people
have decided to call me.

00:20:01.890 --> 00:20:03.030
It's not who I am.

00:20:03.030 --> 00:20:05.160
- Okay, so this adds a
really interesting wrinkle,

00:20:05.160 --> 00:20:08.880
because trademark is not
the end of this discussion.

00:20:08.880 --> 00:20:12.150
Often, people have publicity
rights in their name

00:20:12.150 --> 00:20:13.890
if you have a certain amount of notoriety.

00:20:13.890 --> 00:20:18.060
People like Beyonce and
Donald Trump have trademarked

00:20:18.060 --> 00:20:21.100
and also created other
protections in their own name

00:20:22.650 --> 00:20:23.820
to protect it from

00:20:23.820 --> 00:20:26.854
being diluted or tarnished
by someone else's use,

00:20:26.854 --> 00:20:28.710
even as a trademark.

00:20:28.710 --> 00:20:30.690
- GLK &amp; H made She-Hulk

00:20:30.690 --> 00:20:33.390
the face of our superhuman law division,

00:20:33.390 --> 00:20:35.370
and now we find ourselves

00:20:35.370 --> 00:20:37.353
in the middle of a frivolous lawsuit.

00:20:38.820 --> 00:20:41.880
No one hires a firm that
can't handle its own messes.

00:20:41.880 --> 00:20:44.320
- I am going to fix this, sir.

00:20:44.320 --> 00:20:45.420
(Holden chuckles)

00:20:45.420 --> 00:20:46.770
- No, you won't.

00:20:46.770 --> 00:20:48.150
- Okay, this is great.

00:20:48.150 --> 00:20:49.491
This absolutely would happen.

00:20:49.491 --> 00:20:53.070
If you had a partner in a major law firm

00:20:53.070 --> 00:20:55.470
who was being sued in a frivolous fashion,

00:20:55.470 --> 00:20:59.670
it is absolutely the case that
the other members of the firm

00:20:59.670 --> 00:21:02.670
would just unleash hell on the party

00:21:02.670 --> 00:21:04.800
that was engaging in
the frivolous lawsuit.

00:21:04.800 --> 00:21:08.644
So, it's really funny that the
senior partner is kinda like,

00:21:08.644 --> 00:21:10.620
it's a bad look for us to be sued

00:21:10.620 --> 00:21:12.510
and to lose this kind of thing,

00:21:12.510 --> 00:21:14.070
so we're gonna make sure that you win.

00:21:14.070 --> 00:21:15.720
- Since the first public appearance

00:21:15.720 --> 00:21:18.570
of Jennifer Walters in her Hulk form,

00:21:18.570 --> 00:21:21.008
the public has unanimously identified her

00:21:21.008 --> 00:21:24.402
with the name She-Hulk.

00:21:24.402 --> 00:21:26.160
In fact, the name She-Hulk

00:21:26.160 --> 00:21:28.380
had not even been recorded
in public discourse

00:21:28.380 --> 00:21:29.640
until that debut.

00:21:29.640 --> 00:21:31.710
- Okay, by the way, maybe a small thing,

00:21:31.710 --> 00:21:35.490
but I know TV shows portray
the practice of law as

00:21:35.490 --> 00:21:39.240
basically just two lawyers at
a table arguing with a judge.

00:21:39.240 --> 00:21:41.820
That's not how a summary
judgment motion works.

00:21:41.820 --> 00:21:43.230
A summary judgment motion, number one,

00:21:43.230 --> 00:21:46.110
has to be noticed 75 days ahead of time.

00:21:46.110 --> 00:21:48.000
In California, you have to
write a separate statement.

00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:50.670
So you have to compile
every single relevant fact,

00:21:50.670 --> 00:21:52.830
put it into an Excel spreadsheet,

00:21:52.830 --> 00:21:55.853
and show how there are no
issues of material fact

00:21:55.853 --> 00:21:57.450
that are in dispute,

00:21:57.450 --> 00:22:00.510
and then you have to have 30 pages of law

00:22:00.510 --> 00:22:03.330
explaining why you're entitled
to win as a matter of law.

00:22:03.330 --> 00:22:05.220
It's such a paper-intensive thing.

00:22:05.220 --> 00:22:07.350
It would be hundreds
and hundreds of pages,

00:22:07.350 --> 00:22:09.120
though I can see why a TV show

00:22:09.120 --> 00:22:11.520
would not want to portray a
real motion for summary judgment

00:22:11.520 --> 00:22:14.130
because it's super, super boring.

00:22:14.130 --> 00:22:16.050
- The defendant is exploiting goodwill

00:22:16.050 --> 00:22:18.390
towards my client and
her better-known cousin

00:22:18.390 --> 00:22:23.390
in order to sell her
non-FDA-approved sham products.

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:26.820
We ask the court to grant our
motion for summary judgment

00:22:26.820 --> 00:22:28.740
and order our requested remedies.

00:22:28.740 --> 00:22:29.820
- That makes no sense.

00:22:29.820 --> 00:22:31.710
They should have gone in for
a temporary restraining order,

00:22:31.710 --> 00:22:35.700
a TRO, and eventually a
preliminary injunction, a PI,

00:22:35.700 --> 00:22:37.950
because when you're dealing
with these kinds of IP rights,

00:22:37.950 --> 00:22:39.540
this is where you get irreparable harm,

00:22:39.540 --> 00:22:41.990
which is what is required for a TRO

00:22:41.990 --> 00:22:44.970
to restrain someone temporarily.

00:22:44.970 --> 00:22:46.380
You have to show irreparable harm,

00:22:46.380 --> 00:22:48.510
and when you're dealing with
these kinds of IP rights,

00:22:48.510 --> 00:22:50.460
they're really tarnishing the name,

00:22:50.460 --> 00:22:53.280
and it's really hard, if not
impossible, to get that back.

00:22:53.280 --> 00:22:55.350
They're tarnishing the name She-Hulk

00:22:55.350 --> 00:22:57.991
by associating it with
shoddy beauty products.

00:22:57.991 --> 00:22:59.880
That would absolutely be the sort of thing

00:22:59.880 --> 00:23:02.956
that would be stopped pending
a resolution in this case.

00:23:02.956 --> 00:23:06.240
- Okay, first of all, sham?

00:23:06.240 --> 00:23:07.713
- Why is the client talking?

00:23:08.610 --> 00:23:09.690
Assuming this is an oral argument,

00:23:09.690 --> 00:23:11.940
the lawyer is the only one
who can talk, not the client.

00:23:11.940 --> 00:23:12.960
Sit down and shut up.

00:23:12.960 --> 00:23:13.793
That's no.

00:23:13.793 --> 00:23:16.110
- She-Hulk was not. You can sit down.

00:23:16.110 --> 00:23:17.829
What my client is trying to say is that

00:23:17.829 --> 00:23:21.100
Jennifer Walters was not
interested in the She-Hulk name

00:23:21.100 --> 00:23:22.860
until it became associated with

00:23:22.860 --> 00:23:26.550
this incredibly successful
line of products.

00:23:26.550 --> 00:23:27.626
If we may.

00:23:27.626 --> 00:23:29.550
(crowd chattering)

00:23:29.550 --> 00:23:30.810
- I will not answer to that name.

00:23:30.810 --> 00:23:33.210
You know why? Because it's not my name.

00:23:33.210 --> 00:23:35.580
- Yeah, okay, so that's a good argument

00:23:35.580 --> 00:23:38.010
that this is a bonafide use in commerce.

00:23:38.010 --> 00:23:39.690
She is not using it in commerce,

00:23:39.690 --> 00:23:42.510
and therefore the the
trademark is successful.

00:23:42.510 --> 00:23:44.220
Now, a couple things here.

00:23:44.220 --> 00:23:46.430
There's a difference
between creating a trademark

00:23:46.430 --> 00:23:48.780
and registering a trademark.

00:23:48.780 --> 00:23:49.650
To register a trademark,

00:23:49.650 --> 00:23:52.170
you have to go to the
USPTO to register it.

00:23:52.170 --> 00:23:53.730
Usually, that takes a couple of years,

00:23:53.730 --> 00:23:55.410
assuming that there aren't any objections,

00:23:55.410 --> 00:23:57.960
and then it is registered with
the United States government

00:23:57.960 --> 00:23:59.493
and then you can file suit.

00:24:00.930 --> 00:24:02.430
Generally speaking,
you have to register it

00:24:02.430 --> 00:24:03.960
to be able to file suit,

00:24:03.960 --> 00:24:05.880
but that's not how you create a trademark.

00:24:05.880 --> 00:24:08.610
You create a trademark
by using it in commerce,

00:24:08.610 --> 00:24:10.290
and just because it's not registered

00:24:10.290 --> 00:24:12.030
doesn't mean you don't have the trademark,

00:24:12.030 --> 00:24:13.650
so there's an issue here as to,

00:24:13.650 --> 00:24:17.430
who was the first to use
the mark in commerce?

00:24:17.430 --> 00:24:18.360
That's who's going to win,

00:24:18.360 --> 00:24:20.460
not the person who was
able to register it first.

00:24:20.460 --> 00:24:23.340
- Because I made a dating
profile as She-Hulk,

00:24:23.340 --> 00:24:25.920
and when on several dates as She-Hulk,

00:24:25.920 --> 00:24:28.830
showing a clear pattern of using the name.

00:24:28.830 --> 00:24:31.710
Every single one of those dates
is a corroborating witness.

00:24:31.710 --> 00:24:35.677
This is how I win this case.

00:24:35.677 --> 00:24:38.040
- I specifically remember her

00:24:38.040 --> 00:24:40.470
referring to herself as She-Hulk.

00:24:40.470 --> 00:24:43.260
- Okay, so I understand
this as a plot device.

00:24:43.260 --> 00:24:46.050
It's fun that they're gonna
call in all the ex-boyfriends,

00:24:46.050 --> 00:24:47.490
and, you know, show that

00:24:47.490 --> 00:24:48.990
she actually went by She-Hulk.

00:24:48.990 --> 00:24:51.870
The thing is that is not
probative of the issue here.

00:24:51.870 --> 00:24:54.270
She has to show use in commerce.

00:24:54.270 --> 00:24:57.083
Showing that you used the mark
She-Hulk in a dating profile

00:24:57.083 --> 00:24:59.700
is not using that in commerce.

00:24:59.700 --> 00:25:00.870
That's not going to help them,

00:25:00.870 --> 00:25:03.372
so all of these witnesses
are completely irrelevant.

00:25:03.372 --> 00:25:05.640
They should object and never
be let into the courtroom.

00:25:05.640 --> 00:25:06.900
Now, that being said,

00:25:06.900 --> 00:25:09.270
the best argument that she
could make here is that

00:25:09.270 --> 00:25:11.970
she was hired on the
basis of being She-Hulk.

00:25:11.970 --> 00:25:15.570
- And I want you, well, the
She-Hulk, to be the face of it.

00:25:15.570 --> 00:25:19.410
- She has held herself out
as She-Hulk the lawyer,

00:25:19.410 --> 00:25:21.840
so the evidence that
they should use here is

00:25:21.840 --> 00:25:23.910
calling her clients to the stand,

00:25:23.910 --> 00:25:25.740
showing that they hired her

00:25:25.740 --> 00:25:28.410
on the basis of the fact
that she was She-Hulk

00:25:28.410 --> 00:25:30.660
and she was portraying
herself as She-Hulk.

00:25:30.660 --> 00:25:33.180
- Motion for summary judgment is granted.

00:25:33.180 --> 00:25:36.060
Titania and her subsidiaries will cease

00:25:36.060 --> 00:25:39.180
usage of the She-Hulk name,
effective immediately.

00:25:39.180 --> 00:25:40.920
- It makes sense that
Jennifer Walters wins.

00:25:40.920 --> 00:25:42.540
I wish they would've
dealt with the issue of

00:25:42.540 --> 00:25:44.850
a truly altruistic superhero though.

00:25:44.850 --> 00:25:49.290
Superman, Spider-Man,
Captain America, you know,

00:25:49.290 --> 00:25:50.880
they're not going to be using

00:25:50.880 --> 00:25:54.450
their particular marks or
their names in commerce,

00:25:54.450 --> 00:25:56.280
so it would've been more fun

00:25:56.280 --> 00:25:57.598
to deal with that particular issue.

00:25:57.598 --> 00:25:58.431
(superhero transition music)

00:25:58.431 --> 00:26:00.600
- Oh, man, then you've gotta
read "Ultimate Spider-Man."

00:26:00.600 --> 00:26:01.433
They did a thing where

00:26:01.433 --> 00:26:02.880
Kingpin bought the wrestling company

00:26:02.880 --> 00:26:05.610
where Peter Parker debuted as Spider-Man,

00:26:05.610 --> 00:26:09.360
so Kingpin now owns the rights
to the Spider-Man trademark

00:26:09.360 --> 00:26:10.470
and uses it to sell

00:26:10.470 --> 00:26:12.870
all of the licensed
Spider-Man merchandise.

00:26:12.870 --> 00:26:15.780
It's pretty genius, although
I guess it only really works

00:26:15.780 --> 00:26:17.940
if your hero has a secret identity

00:26:17.940 --> 00:26:19.530
and cannot claim the trademark themselves.

00:26:19.530 --> 00:26:20.363
(superhero transition music)

00:26:20.363 --> 00:26:22.620
- Okay, that was the first
five episodes of "She-Hulk."

00:26:22.620 --> 00:26:24.608
Now it's time to give it
a grade for legal realism.

00:26:24.608 --> 00:26:27.191
(lively music)

00:26:29.220 --> 00:26:31.260
Man, I wanted to like it.

00:26:31.260 --> 00:26:32.190
There's some funny jokes,

00:26:32.190 --> 00:26:34.080
if for no other reason than the line-

00:26:34.080 --> 00:26:36.330
- Captain America!

00:26:36.330 --> 00:26:37.950
- They did cover some legal issues

00:26:37.950 --> 00:26:40.320
that exclusively would've
come up in a superhero world

00:26:40.320 --> 00:26:43.110
and dealt with them in a
way that is quasi-realistic.

00:26:43.110 --> 00:26:46.200
That being said, the courtroom
scenes are complete nonsense.

00:26:46.200 --> 00:26:49.380
All of the legal terminology
is just complete word salad,

00:26:49.380 --> 00:26:51.000
so, sadly, I'm gonna
have to give "She-Hulk"

00:26:51.000 --> 00:26:53.220
a C for legal realism.

00:26:53.220 --> 00:26:56.220
Legal inaccuracies make Devin mad.

00:26:56.220 --> 00:26:57.510
Yeah, so the bottom line here is that

00:26:57.510 --> 00:26:59.730
some of these clients got royally
screwed by their attorney.

